< {"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_iVMdR7hI6MYQ227ogh1rk5Q7|fc_00e81a80824e53fb016ab7514caf4c87d0865929e3cb7b5885","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python - <<'PY'\np='''Chicago’s zoning history reflects the city’s changing approach to growth, industry, and neighborhood life. The 1909 Plan of Chicago called for orderly expansion, parks, and grand boulevards, but did not itself create zoning law. Chicago adopted its first comprehensive zoning ordinance in 1923, dividing land into residential, commercial, and industrial districts and regulating building height, bulk, and yards. This framework emerged during rapid industrialization, when residents and businesses sought to limit incompatible uses and control development. Although the Supreme Court had barred explicit racial zoning in 1917, segregation persisted through restrictive covenants, discriminatory lending, and land-use rules that shaped where housing could be built. A major ordinance revision took effect in 1957, amid urban renewal, highway construction, and suburban growth. After decades of piecemeal amendments, Chicago adopted a substantially rewritten zoning code in 2004. Later reforms supported denser development near transit and expanded mixed-use possibilities, while debates over affordability, displacement, and neighborhood control continued to define zoning politics.'''\nprint(len(p.split()))\nfor i,w in enumerate(p.split(),1): print(i,w)\nPY","timeout":null}}}}
