< {"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ILvRv1UP50LhHqlpmazg62IK|fc_00e81a80824e53fb016ab7516dac4887d0b575340efac3d682","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python - <<'PY'\np='''Houston is often described as the largest American city without conventional zoning, but its growth is not unregulated. Voters rejected citywide zoning proposals in 1948, 1962, and 1993, leaving the city without a comprehensive map separating residential, commercial, and industrial uses. Instead, development is shaped by subdivision rules, building and fire codes, parking requirements, minimum lot sizes, and private deed restrictions. These tools can strongly limit what gets built: deed restrictions may reserve neighborhoods for particular uses, while minimum-lot rules influence housing density. Because there is no general zoning barrier to mixing uses, shops, apartments, offices, and industry can sometimes stand closer together than in zoned cities. This flexibility has helped accommodate rapid growth and commercial development, but it can also expose homes to nearby traffic, industry, or incompatible projects. Houston’s system thus shifts much land-use control from a citywide zoning map to parcel regulations, private covenants, and political bargaining. Its history illustrates that “no zoning” means a different regulatory structure, not an absence of rules.'''\nprint(len(p.split()))\nPY","timeout":null}}}}
