5932f9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"python - <<'PY'\np='''Houston is often called the largest U.S. city without conventional zoning, but it is not unregulated. Voters rejected zoning proposals in 1948, 1962, and 1993, leaving no citywide map separating residential, commercial, and industrial uses. Instead, development is shaped by subdivision rules, building and fire codes, parking requirements, minimum lot sizes, and private deed restrictions. These tools limit what gets built: deed restrictions may reserve neighborhoods for particular uses, while minimum-lot rules influence housing density. Without general zoning barriers to mixing uses, shops, apartments, offices, and industry can sometimes stand closer together than in zoned cities. Flexibility helps accommodate rapid growth and commercial development, but can expose homes to nearby traffic, industry, or incompatible projects. Houston’s system shifts land-use control from a zoning map to parcel regulations, private covenants, and political bargaining. Its history shows that “no zoning” means a different regulatory structure, not no rules.'''\nprint(len(p.split()))\nfor i,w in enumerate(p.split(),1): print(i,w)\nPY","timeout":null}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-6-luna","usage":{"input":985,"output":612,"cacheRead":6656,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":358,"totalTokens":8253,"cost":{"input":0.00009850000000000001,"output":0.000306,"cacheRead":0.00006656,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00047106000000000003}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790398832866,"responseId":"resp_00e81a80824e53fb016ab7517114f887d096b55067b64d2fe4","rawStopReason":"completed"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IIoVKi6Nffh1a4ORnyFqsnDJ|fc_00e81a80824e53fb016ab7517a7adc87d095564db2af5932f9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"146\n1 Houston\n2 is\n3 often\n4 called\n5 the\n6 largest\n7 U.S.\n8 city\n9 without\n10 conventional\n11 zoning,\n12 but\n13 it\n14 is\n15 n